From a06618f7402b670206c8a85ca2c81a0e2932d212 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ahmad Mahmoud <alshahia@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 15:36:44 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Q2 (perf/queue-controls-gpu-codec): Pause / Resume /
 Cancel buttons
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds a user-facing control channel between the GUI and PipelineWorker
so in-flight jobs can be paused, resumed, or cancelled from the queue
listbox. No worker API breakage: Phase A1's ctl_queue argument is
optional (defaults to a private queue.Queue() per worker), so existing
tests that construct PipelineWorker(in_q, out_q) still pass.

State machine
-------------
JobStatus extends with PAUSED = 'paused'. CANCELLED was already
present and is reused for the cancel terminal state.

  pending  --start-->  running  --pause-->  paused  --resume-->  running
                              |                                  |
                              +-----cancel---->  cancelled <-----+

Worker contract
---------------
* New JobControlEvent(kind, job_id) dataclass in pipeline.jobs
  (kind ∈ {'pause','resume','cancel'}, default job_id=0).
* PipelineWorker.__init__ accepts an optional ctl_queue argument;
  when omitted, a private queue.Queue() is synthesised so the legacy
  2-arg constructor still works (Phase A1 contract).
* send_control(evt): GUI-facing push helper (put to ctl_queue).
* _drain_ctl_for_job(job_id): non-blocking drain -- pause arms the
  per-job threading.Event, resume clears it, cancel raises
  _JobCancelled. Events for other job_ids are re-enqueued in FIFO
  order so a slow pause for job 99 doesn't delay cancel for job 42.
* _check_pause(job_id): blocks on the per-job Event in 0.1s slices
  polling the ctl_queue so cancel can interrupt a pause.
* _run_image and _run_video drain controls at job start; _run_video
  also drains + checks pause at the frame boundary (after the
  skip-rest check) so the current frame finishes writing and MP4
  streams stay consistent.
* _run_one wraps dispatch in try/except _JobCancelled: deletes the
  partial output file and emits JobEvent(kind='cancelled'); finally
  calls _finalize_job so the per-job pause Event is removed.

GUI contract
------------
* widgets/input_panel.py gains a Pause/Resume/Cancel row above the
  Filter row. Buttons default to state='disabled' and enable based
  on the selected job's status:
    Pause   enabled iff status == RUNNING
    Resume  enabled iff status == PAUSED
    Cancel  enabled iff status ∈ {RUNNING, PAUSED}
  A new _selected_job_id() returns the first selected row's id (or 0).
  _refresh_controls() is called from App._refresh_queue_listbox so
  selection moves update the buttons without an extra round-trip.

* App._send_pause / _send_resume / _send_cancel push the JobControlEvent
  and flip the local status optimistically so the listbox + buttons
  update immediately. Cancel shows a confirmation dialog
  ('cancelled by user') and sets job_rec.error so the message sticks
  even if the worker never confirms.

* App._handle_event learns a new 'cancelled' branch that mirrors
